<<~ ahu #/head>>

! The Sensorium Runbook ~ the tending rite

> Status: PROPOSED. The verb surface below stands VERIFIED against the MCP roster (2026-08-04); the
> command spellings stand PARTLY verified and each one says which. No end-to-end tending cycle has run
> against this document. ''Where a run contradicts a movement here, the run wins'' and the movement gets
> rewritten — the same self-amending law the founding rite carries.

''Two rites stand, and they never merge.'' The founding rite consecrates: irreversible, once, governed by
quorum and custody and succession. This rite tends: repeatable, operational, governed by measurement and
cadence and capacity.

''The distinction that keeps them apart reads as a difference in
cardinal sin.'' A founding's worst outcome ''loses a key'' and nothing recovers it. A sensorium's worst
outcome ''loses a baseline'' — and while the corpus always re-pours from its sources, ''a measurement never
taken cannot be taken later''. A rite built around irreversibility teaches the wrong reflexes for a thing
whose recovery move reads //do it again, watching//.

''The lore already names this rite.'' A lararium stabilizes THROUGH USE — fed daily at the shrine, food and
drink and incense and coin; a fed node hums and a neglected one flickers (#/noosphere-boot). The founding rite runs
the consecration. ''This one runs the daily offering.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-planes>>

!! Ⓐ Stand it up ~ sources become planes

''A sensorium holds planes, and a plane holds nothing until something feeds it.'' The instruments read
whatever stands; against an unfed plane they answer honestly and say nothing, which reads identical to a
broken instrument unless the operator knows which planes carry weight.

!!! Where a sensorium stands ~ the house's own home

''THREE WORDS, AND NONE OF THEM ARE SYNONYMS'': ''lares'' names the SPIRITS (agents, personas — their
identity, and the vessel substrate they run on), ''lararium'' names the HOUSE (the acquired shelf, the
sensoriums, the history), and ''lar'' names the NAMESPACE, the `lar:///…` URIs and every resolver in the
stack. ''Every siting answers one question — whose is this?'' A sensorium holds operator history and
DreamNet history, so it belongs to the HOUSE, and it stands in the house's home:

|! form |! the sensorium root |! resolver |
| the operator's disk | `~/.local/share/lararium/sensoriums/<name>` | `larariumDataHome()` · `_lararium_data_home()` |
| an isolated root (`LAR_ROOT`) | `<root>/data/lararium/sensoriums/<name>` | the same two, re-prefixed |

Under an isolated root each directory names an XDG ''KIND'' — data, state, cache, config, run — and the two
HOUSES nest inside the data kind exactly as they do under XDG: `<root>/data/lares` beside
`<root>/data/lararium`. ''A house never stands where a kind belongs'', so an isolated root reads as the real
disk with a different prefix and a rehearsal never reaches the operator's own planes. Both tongues resolve
the same string — the TypeScript `larariumDataHome()` and the Python `_lararium_data_home()` — so a holder
started from either side opens the same store.

''What a rite re-makes decides nothing here; whose it is decides everything.''
A Lar wakes, acts and gives way, and `clear`, `bake` and `rebirth` reforge the substrate beneath it whole.
None of them reach a sensorium, because a sensorium never stood in the spirits' home to begin with. That it
also survives every founding reads as the ''CONSEQUENCE'' of belonging to the house, never the reason — and
belonging makes the protection STRUCTURAL: a tier kept safe by its absence from a wipe-list stays safe until
somebody widens the list by one path segment, while a tier standing in its own house leaves no edge to widen.

!!! What feeds what ~ four planes over four bases

''Read this table off `plane_base.PLANE_BASE`, never off memory.'' The roster below states what that map
declares as of 2026-08-04; a plane absent from it ''has no declared base, and no instrument may read it
cross-plane'' — which the code enforces rather than requests.

| plane | eats | keys on | stands as |
|---|---|---|---|
| ''content'' | transcripts, project files, guest harvests | a drawer cid (RECORD) | a sheaf — what a RECORD says |
| ''form'' | the same streams, encoded by shape | a drawer cid (RECORD) | a sheaf — a membership subset of the induced constructicon |
| ''structure'' | parse trees (`.structurepalace`) | a structural hash (PATTERN) | a cosheaf-shaped registry — what a SHAPE does across the corpus |
| ''persistence'' | testimony claims | a claim cid (CLAIM) | a sheaf over the claim base |

''A structure row and a content row live over different
universes.'' A sup-norm between them reads a distance between two worlds and returns a number anyway.
''The only road across'' runs the extension map — a structure row's provenance lists the records
exhibiting that pattern, and the pushforward carries two facts an ad-hoc crossing hides: ''the fiber
reads as a SET'' (one record may exhibit many patterns, so a caller wanting one must say which and eat
the loss aloud), and ''the map runs lossy by design'' (provenance truncates past a cap, so a hot
pattern pushes forward onto strictly fewer records than exhibit it).

''`require_base()` refuses a restriction set that mixes bases or omits one'', so the wrong comparison
stops type-checking rather than returning a plausible figure.

<<~ set hud="yield" drift-ward="! · ⚠ · this table stood hand-written in an earlier draft, with a plane the code does not
declare and a base it does · ↻ L-Prime">>

!!! The order that matters

# ''Sources first, and verified present.'' Every drawer must trace to a source that still stands on disk.
  A pour from a source set that lost a member yields a corpus whose gaps read as SIGNAL to every
  instrument downstream.
# ''Content plane before structure.'' Structure keys on a parse of what content holds; a structure pour
  ahead of its content produces hashes with nothing to bind to.
# ''Stamp the embedder identity at birth.'' A palace that carries no recorded embedder id cannot be
  checked against a later one. See #catastrophe, the embedder-mismatch drill — this stamp reads as the
  cheapest cure in the whole rite and it stays available ''only at pour time''.
# ''Measure before the first byte lands.'' #teardown carries the law.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/feed>>

!! Ⓑ Feed it ~ the cadence, and what starvation looks like first

''Live capture and a bulk sweep race each other.'' The rite therefore pauses live capture before a bulk
pour and resumes it after — a sweep that runs against a moving source set produces a corpus no second run
reproduces, which quietly destroys the comparability the whole instrument rests on.

''A starved plane looks HEALTHY before it looks broken.'' Its instruments keep answering; the answers just
narrow. The tells, in the order they appear:

* ''Entity counts plateau'' while sessions keep landing — the extractor stopped reaching new ground.
* ''Cross-plane queries collapse to one plane's hits'' — the widening finds nothing to widen INTO.
* ''Coupling reads honest-insufficient'' rather than low — the instrument names its own starvation, and an
  operator who reads that as a coupling VERDICT has read a missing plane as a finding.

''Honest-insufficient reads as the instrument working.'' It carries more
information than a number would; a gauge that returned 0.0 against an unfed plane would have lied.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/read>>

!! Ⓒ Read it ~ what a figure claims, and over what

''Every reading names a plane and a grain, or it names nothing.'' The verbs verified on the MCP roster
(2026-08-04): `li` · `ki` · `jing` · `pour` · `sweep` · `recall` · `analyze` · `rejim` · `worldline` ·
`flow` · `forecast` · `couple_r` · `mismatch` · `plane_record` · `reconcile` · `status`.

!!! The reading families

* ''SHAPE'' — `status` (wings · rooms · entities · totals: what landed) · `plane_record` (a plane's own
  ledger). The first question after any pour.
* ''RECALL'' — `recall` and search across lenses; a single lens reads one stratum, a cross-plane lens
  WIDENS one hit across content ∪ form ∪ structure.
* ''GEOLOGY'' — `rejim` (detected rhythm and regimes) · `analyze` (change-point arms: where content
  changes hands). Both read the POURED corpus, so both want running after a sweep completes rather than
  during.
* ''COUPLING'' — `li` · `ki` · `couple_r` · `mismatch`. These carry the entropy/negentropy reading the
  outside tooling wants, and they read ACROSS planes — so each one answers only as well as its thinnest
  plane is fed.
* ''SELF'' — `jing` extends the lobes to a reconciled self, restricts back, and coheres iff the round-trip
  equals the identity. A round-trip that fails names a real incoherence rather than a bug.
* ''LINEAGE'' — `worldline` (the fork-DAG: parent → spirit spawn → handback).

!!! The one instruction that governs all of them

''Name the plane and the grain before reading the number.'' A figure detached from its partition reads as a
fact about the world when it names a fact about a choice (#/capacity).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/catastrophe>>

!! Ⓓ Catastrophe & recovery ~ six drills, each with its detector

''A drill without a detector reads as reassurance.'' Each entry below names how the operator SEES it, then
what to do — and the seeing comes first, because every one of these presents as //the sensorium feels
slightly off// long before it presents as a failure.

!!! ① The poisoned pour ~ one bad source contaminates every plane

* ''Detector'' — an entity or wing whose counts sit orders off its neighbours; a regime boundary that lands
  exactly on an ingest boundary rather than on anything in the content.
* ''Cure'' — identify the source, drop its drawers by `source_file`, re-pour that source alone. The pour
  runs idempotent, so a narrowed re-run skips what already landed.
* ''The trap'' — a re-pour of EVERYTHING to be safe destroys the before/after boundary that would have
  proven the cure worked.

!!! ② The wedged holder ~ handles accumulate until nothing opens

* ''Detector'' — open file handles climbing across a long run while work does not. Sample the count
  periodically DURING a pour; a leak reveals itself as a slope, never as a value.
* ''Cure'' — drain the holders and restart them; the corpus takes no harm.
* ''Live'' — this stack currently carries a measured leak (a peak of 593
  against 1245 at an older wedge point) whose route stands UNKNOWN. A hypothesis that named one file
  rested on that file's own docstring rather than on its code, and dissolved on reading. ''Treat the next
  long pour as the experiment that finds it.''

!!! ③ The corrupted index ~ the vectors disagree with the record

* ''Detector'' — searches return hits whose payloads do not match their queries; counts diverge between
  the record and the index.
* ''Cure'' — ''sqlite stands as source-of-truth''; rebuild the index from it. The index reads as derived
  and therefore disposable.
* ''The trap'' — repairing the record to match the index inverts the authority and makes the corruption
  canonical.

!!! ④ The embedder mismatch ~ two vector spaces, silently incomparable

* ''Detector'' — a warning that a collection carries no recorded embedder id, or a recorded id that
  differs from the driver's. ''Recall quality degrades without any error at all'' — this failure has no
  loud form.
* ''Cure'' — re-pour under a single declared embedder, stamping identity at birth.
* ''Live'' — the standing content palace warns `EmbedderIdentityUnknownWarning`; its guards (`expected_dim`,
  `expected_model`, the palace model-history assertion) have ''nothing to check against''. The
  same-dimension different-model swap clears a dimension check while corrupting recall, which is
  precisely why the model pin exists beside the dimension pin.

!!! ⑤ The interrupted sweep ~ a partial pour that reads complete

* ''Detector'' — per-surface tallies that stop short of their source counts; a wing whose newest drawer
  predates the newest source.
* ''Cure'' — re-run; the pour skips what landed. ''Chunk by surface'' when a single call outruns its
  caller's patience — the work continues in the holder even when the caller returns early, so ''a caller
  timeout NEVER proves the work stopped''.
* ''The trap'' — reading a caller's early return as a failure and clearing the store to start clean.

!!! ⑥ The lost baseline ~ the only one that does not recover

* ''Detector'' — none. By construction. It presents as a question nobody can answer later.
* ''Cure'' — none after the fact. ''Prevention only'': take the reading before the change.
* ''Why it ranks first in severity and last in visibility'' — every other entry here recovers by
  re-pouring. This one names the class of loss the corpus's re-derivability cannot cover, and it is the
  reason this rite exists apart from the founding rite at all.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/teardown>>

!! Ⓔ Tear down & re-sweep ~ the deliberate reset

''A teardown reads as a legitimate move, not a failure.'' A corpus accumulates provenance debt — mixed
embedders, half-migrated schemas, sources long since renamed — and past some point a fresh pour under one
declared regime costs less than reconciling the drift.

!!! The measure-first law

''ONE clean before/after boundary exists per re-pour, and it opens
exactly once.'' Instrument BEFORE the teardown or the comparison never becomes available. A pour
instrumented after the fact requires a second pour to measure, which costs the whole run again and still
lacks the original state to compare against.

!!! The preflight ~ what a teardown makes unrecoverable

Run every check before the first deletion:

# ''Sources present.'' Every stream the corpus drew from still stands on disk, at its expected size.
# ''Nothing authored-only.'' Any derived artifact (hallway graphs, entity links, co-occurrence records)
  MUST carry a marker distinguishing auto-derived entries from hand-authored ones. ''Auto-derived
  re-pours; hand-authored does not.'' A store that cannot answer this question has not earned a teardown.
# ''The write log drained.'' Confirm the log holds COMPLETED operations (records carrying results) rather
  than a pending queue. A pending entry names work that never landed anywhere else.
# ''The old provenance kept.'' Copy the write log out before the teardown — not for recovery, but as the
  baseline the new pour gets compared against. It costs kilobytes.
# ''The operator's hand named the store.'' `lares sense teardown`, `purge` and `rederive.py` reach into
  `<lararium>/sensoriums` ''on purpose'' — a dev-time re-pour wants exactly that, and no guard belongs on
  them. The structural protection covers the machine's rites, never the operator's own hand, so the reading
  the operator must not lose stands at #catastrophe ⑥: capture the machine took once and cannot take again.

!!! The movement

''THE MOVEMENT RUNS AS ONE COMMAND, and the steps that land
nothing are the reason it is one.'' `lares sense pour --all` walks the sequence below; the steps stand
here because a reader tending a sensorium by hand needs to know what each one buys, and because a step
skipped silently is what the single door exists to prevent.

# ''Pause live capture'' — a bulk sweep and a live hook racing produce an unreproducible corpus.
# ''Take the baseline'' — the before-reading, while it can still be taken (#catastrophe ⑥).
# ''Drain the holders'' before removing anything, so no process holds a handle into a store about to
  vanish.
# ''Remove the store directory'' for a bare slate — a drain releases the holders and leaves every byte
  in place, so a drain alone re-pours ON TOP of what stands. ''This step stays the operator's own hand'':
  it is the one move that destroys, and the rite carries it, never a flag.
# ''Sweep'', detached — a full structure parse across every session runs long, and the per-op budget is
  sized for it rather than for a short read. Per-surface tallies land at the end.
# ''Stamp the bearing'' onto the drawers that landed, then ''re-derive the projection'' over them. The
  order is a dependency: the stamp reads what the sweep wrote.
# ''Verify the shape'' — counts against source counts, before trusting any reading.
# ''Resume live capture'', on every path out. A pass that halted holding the hooks down leaves the
  machine quietly not remembering, which reads as calm rather than as fault.
# ''Take the new baseline'' immediately, against the kept old one.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/capacity>>

!! Ⓕ Capacity & honest limits ~ what a reading CANNOT claim

''This section names the failures the instrument invites when it works correctly.'' A grimoire that omits
the working's limits invites the operator to ask for what it cannot give and to believe the answer.

!!! The ceiling

''`I(target; plane) ≤ H(plane)`'' — exact, and it forbids manufacture. A
plane cannot report more about a target than the plane itself carries. So ''a low reading names a
capacity limit OR an absence, and the figure alone never distinguishes them.'' An operator who reads a
ceiling-bound figure as a finding about the world has read the instrument's own entropy.

!!! The grain law

''Store fine, index coarse — and the bin width stands as an ANALYSIS parameter, never a property of the
data.'' The modifiable areal unit problem holds here exactly: ''the unit manufactures the finding.'' Two
honest analysts partitioning the same corpus differently will report different couplings, both correct
within their partition. Hence #read's rule: name the plane and the grain, or name nothing.

!!! Planes do not share a base space

A cross-plane figure describes a RELATION between differently-typed observations, never a measurement in
one common space. ''Reading it as a single scalar over one substrate mistypes the instrument.''

!!! What no reading claims

* ''Causation.'' A change point names where content changed hands, never why.
* ''Completeness.'' Absence of a finding and absence of looking generate identically.
* ''A verdict on a person.'' The corpus holds acts and faces; a standing gets EVALUATED from a vantage and
  never PUBLISHED as a property (#/identity-enacts-relation).
* ''Reproducibility across a re-pour with different sources.'' Comparability requires a fixed source set;
  a corpus poured from a moved target measures the move.
* ''Its own ground, by name.'' The stores carry no identity for the pour
  a reading stands over — no `pour_id`, no corpus version, nothing a second reading could match against.
  So ''two readings taken across a re-pour compare nothing'', however faithfully each got recorded, and
  the gap sits UPSTREAM of any ledger: a baseline needs a ground it can name before it needs a place to
  live. The pono shape reads DERIVED rather than assigned — the content plane already
  content-addresses its drawers, so a pour's identity can fall out of its own ground (the source set and
  their hashes, a fold over the drawer cids) with nothing declared by fiat and no network consulted.

<<~ set hud="aim" drift-ward="_ · L-Prime · crossing: generation-talk, where federation vocabulary drifts local">>

''AND THE WORDS FOR THIS STAY LOCAL.'' A sensorium's stores ''never federate'' — content, form, structure
and persistence each say so outright; only the meshpalace takes a feed from the network, and an operator
Act alone crosses any of them to a federated surface. So the lifecycle here speaks ''pour · re-pour ·
teardown · sweep'', never //epoch// and never //burn//. An ''epoch'' names a charter's or engine's
shared regime, ratcheting DreamNet-wide. A ''burn'' destroys an identity overlay or a spent credential:
burning an overlay drops a vessel back to anon ''with its own content whole'', and it ''cuts
future-not-history''. A teardown inverts that on every axis — the content dies, the identity stands
untouched, the history goes and the future does not. Borrowing either word imports a topology these
stores do not have, and //burn// imports the opposite of what a teardown performs.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/reconstitution>>

!! Ⓖ Reconstitution ~ what a stranger needs to stand this cold

''The bus factor applies here as it does at the founding.'' A sensorium held in one operator's head reads
as a single point of failure regardless of how well the stores replicate. The reconstitution kit rides as
a movement of this rite rather than a later courtesy:

# ''The source inventory'' — which streams feed which planes, and where they live.
# ''The declared embedder'' — name and dimension, so a rebuild lands in the same vector space.
# ''The partition of record'' — the grain every published figure was computed at. Without it, no prior
  reading can get reproduced or challenged.
# ''The baseline set'' — the readings taken at each pour boundary, kept as the comparison series.
# ''This document'', which names what the instruments claim and what they refuse.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/surface-drift>>

!! The two surfaces ~ how they slice

''NEITHER SURFACE IS A SUBSET OF THE OTHER, and alignment means naming the
slicing rather than widening one to match.'' `lares sense` holds ONE door over one sensorium and carries
its whole lifecycle — stand up, feed, read, tend the holders, the durable seats, the instrument set. The
MCP roster spans SEVERAL CLI doors at once: `vault_*` answers at `lares vault`, `wiki` at `lares wiki`,
`kapae` at `lares edge`. A tool absent from `sense` is not a gap where its own door holds it.

''One rule does hold in one direction'': every MCP tool answers to SOME CLI door, because a tool an agent
may call and an operator may not is a surface the operator cannot audit by hand. The converse stays
deliberately false — the key-holding ceremonies (founding, persona minting, the charter quorum, the vault
beyond a status read) keep the operator's own hand, since MCP speaks stdio-per-client and N sessions would
put N unsynchronised holders on one operator's keys.

''A NAME SHARED IS NOT A MOTION SHARED.'' The two surfaces may spell one verb alike and mean different
work by it, and a witness that matches rosters passes cleanly over exactly that. So a door and its tool
agree on WHAT THEY WRITE, or the difference stands declared where a reader meets it.

!!! The rite's second job ~ a friction detector, not merely a document

''Every command spelling here reads as a TESTABLE CLAIM about a surface.'' That makes this document an
instrument in its own right: walk it end to end against a live node, and each step either runs or names a
friction — a verb reachable at one surface and not another, a flag an operator would reasonably expect,
a flow demanding three doors where the shape wants one.

''So the drift section grows by USE rather than by audit.'' A walked rite that completes without amendment
reports the surfaces aligned; a walked rite that stalls reports exactly where, in the operator's own
vocabulary rather than the implementer's. Two properties follow, and both matter more than the prose:

* ''The findings re-find themselves.'' A surface that drifts back apart surfaces at the same step on the
  next walk — no separate conformance suite to keep in step, and no second hand-kept list to go stale.
* ''The vocabulary stays the operator's.'' A friction named as //this rite could not proceed// carries the
  gap an implementer's inventory of exported symbols never shows: a verb may exist at every layer and
  still leave a flow unwalkable.

''A roster read off source finds what EXISTS and stays blind to what a
flow REQUIRES'' — absence of finding and absence of looking generate identically. So no count stands
transcribed here: `tools/surface-parity-witness.sh` derives both rosters, and the movement below is what
reports whether they carry a flow between them.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

!! Edges

